gs don't go as planned on  set and you have to make changes on the  fly that's completely normal it never  goes 100 as planned also when you're in  production make sure you keep track of  your memory cards your batteries and all  the other kind of resources you will  need for your camera to make sure the  day goes smoothly so you have all your  memory cards and you've got all the  footage on it and you want to make this  video this is where we enter  the editing phase and um there's a lot  of definitions for what people consider  editing but this is what editing  actually is editing is taking your  footage  and chopping it up and playing it in the  sequence the in sequence sandwich  editing see  this is hard editing is no more than  taking your footage chopping it up in in  order and putting that into a sequence  that's all it is just getting it there  to watch now when you're editing you're  not going to add vfx here you're not  going to add color or sound or any of  that kind of stuff you don't want to do  vfx too early on because sometimes the  length of the clips change and that just  can create a big pain for you later  where you have to re-time things and do  you don't even know which shot you're  going to use yeah i mean what the heck  you find that this shot's out of focus  this shot's better etc and you work your  cut down until you have your final edit  still no sound still no effects you  might be putting you know temp sound in  there just in case it's hard to imagine  you're gonna be wanting to get uh  feedback on your work um every step of  the way because other people's opinions  and ideas and thoughts  usually help improve the piece so after  you finish shooting your piece you have  all your raw footage you're going to put  it on your computer pay attention to  what you're doing with the footage you  want to keep it organized and you want  to keep your structure clean so you  create a project folder inside that  project folder you're going to have your  edits you're going to have your music  your sound your vfx files you'll also  have a folder for your footage you can  call it footage you can call whatever  you want just keep it understandable and  put all your footage in there your  editing program will need to know where  the footage is when you start working  with it if it's on your desktop or  sitting on your main hard drive in like  some random folder you're going to lose  it and things are gonna get messy visual  effects alright  so you got your edit you know which  shots are gonna have vfx instead of  exporting your entire sequence you're  going to go shot by shot and only pick  the shots that have vfx you're going to  send those to the program something like  premiere it's automated you can simply  select your shot right click and say  replace with after effects composition  it's really sweet we use the feature all  the time it's awesome but other programs  it's not entirely that simple what you  can do is you can open after effects  import that raw footage file  and you'll set the endpoint in the out  point of that file to match the endpoint  and out point of the file in your edit  basically you want to keep your shots in  the highest quality format possible you  do not want to lose quality having to go  back and forth between these programs  that's silly lose all that quality when  you have to upload it to youtube silly  so as i said earlier there's a lot of  different programs out there and there's  a lot of different ways you can go about  editing your footage but over the years  we've really tried to perfect our system  and i think we have something that works  really fast and really efficient  so we work with in all adobe um we use  adobe premiere for editing we use after  effects for most of our compositing and  visual effects  now when you're editing in premiere you  don't have to do any transcoding you can  pretty much work with any raw file  format that's out there so that makes  that very simple it helps to also have a  faster computer with a good gpu when we  start vfx we like to split up the work  based off of our specializations i tend  to do a lot more like motion graphics  heavy work um so when it comes to bright  lasers and stuff i i do that um nico has  been uh doing a lot more 3d work than i  have so when it comes to you know  lighting and rendering a scene making  sure that's all like  locked in and good he handles that for  me i also handle uh some other  specialized 3d stuff as far as like  doing like liquid simulations and like  object tracking and little things like  that and so when we combine them all  together we have a  really really good uh vfx workflow uh  from start to finish also quick pro tip  for you guys if you know that you're  gonna be doing motion tracking or  stabilization to your footage shoot at a  higher shutter speed to eliminate motion  blur i'd recommend at one over 120 or  higher  so after i've done my effects i need to  export the clip in the highest quality  format i can but something like  uncompressed is way too big and doesn't  even play back in real time  so there's basically two codecs out  there that work for this if you're on a  mac you can use prores it's super high  quality but it still plays back and you  can actually edit with it on your  computer if you're on pc you can use  something called dnxhd which is made by  avid this one's cross-platform first  you'll need quicktime which is free and  you simply do a google search for dnxhd  and you can download that codec and what  this basically is it's a super high  quality codec that will not eliminate  any data from your image so you'll  preserve all that crisp high quality  awesome work you've been doing in your  effects program and you'll bring it back  into your edit so you'll pick your  export settings you pick quicktime  you'll pick avid dnxhd as your video  format and then there's this little  window that pops up that has all these  crazy options here's 