re we know  what sounds we have to match up to the  footage  music since everything's timed and  everything is kind of you know put  together we have a good idea for how the  piece flows and also  we can move on to color grading uh niko  usually handles the initial round of  color grading while i shift my focus to  doing sound design or music once you  have your vfx completely done your edit  is completely locked there's literally  nothing else going into your head  now it's time to color grade what's up  what's up  i can't wait to see potion so first  things first we're going to take your  timeline and you're going to flatten it  rather than having clips all over the  place going to try to flatten everything  down to one video track it'll make color  grading easier we use davinci light it's  actually free you can get it off the  blackmagic website and we'll do another  piece talking about color grading more  in depth but basically at this point i  go through  and i correct any color mistakes i  correct any exposure mistakes and then i  stylize the footage from here i'll  export our master file which is once  again a dnxhd file which has both the  video and the sound  and then from that master file i'll make  any other exports i need to make such as  h.264 exports for youtube so you're  doing your final h.264 settings for  youtube there's a couple of specific  settings you'll want to pick  first off your bit rate is important you  have the option options between vbr and  cbr variable bit rate or constant bit  rate constant bit rate gives you the  most consistent performance so the size  is a little bit bigger we recommend at  least 8 000 kilobits per second or  higher we actually export most of our  pieces at 13 megabits per second aka 13  000 kilobits per second audio can  basically be encoded into an mp4 format  which is also referred to as aac it's  very similar to mp3 128 kilobits per  second or higher it's totally fine for  your good audio so i hope you guys  l